WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.514
Think of a café menu. Some items sell every single day. Some are new and still finding fans. Some hardly ever sell. All the products a business sells are called its product portfolio.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:33.578
The Boston matrix sorts these products using two measures. Market growth: is the market getting bigger? And market share: how much of that market does the product have? Growth runs up the side, and share runs along the bottom, with high share on the left.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:55.208
Step one: place the product by asking two questions. Is market growth high or low? Is market share high or low? Step two: name the category and its decision. High growth and high share makes a star. Stars are usually worth investing in.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:03.536
Low growth but high share makes a cash cow. It brings in steady profit, which can fund other products.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:17.209
High growth but low share makes a question mark, sometimes called a problem child. Its future is uncertain. It may need investment to succeed, or it may be dropped.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:23.503
Low growth and low share makes a dog. Dogs are often dropped or replaced.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:35.726
Step three: link the decision to the whole portfolio. Profit from cash cows funds stars, question marks and new products, so the range stays balanced across several categories.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:51.262
Here is a real question from Paper one in June 2022. Explain one benefit to a business of using the Boston matrix. It is worth three marks: one benefit, then two linked steps.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:58.530
Mark one is the benefit. The Boston matrix helps a business keep a balanced product portfolio.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:11.409
Mark two shows what the matrix reveals. Managers can see if they rely too heavily on a few cash cows, with too few stars or question marks coming through.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:23.010
Mark three gives the action and result. The business uses cash cow profit to launch new products before older ones fall away, so its income stays steady.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:37.582
Mistake one: swapping the stars and cash cows. Both have high market share. The difference is growth: stars are in growing markets, cash cows are not.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:45.288
Mistake two: thinking the matrix shows profit directly. It measures market share and market growth.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:03:02.657
Now you try. A games company has a football game with a large share of a market that has stopped growing. Where does it go on the Boston matrix, and what should the company do with it? Pause and decide.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:16.392
Low growth and high share make it a cash cow. The company should keep selling it and use its steady profit to develop new games, perhaps for faster-growing markets.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:25.482
Remember the method: ask about growth, ask about share, then name the category and its decision. Now try the practice questions.
